  completeExplanation: 'Curiosity Chapter 10 builds a cohesive biological framework for what defines life. Beginning with Avadhi and Aayush examining a garden snail shell, the chapter explains that an organism does not need to be constantly in motion to be alive. Life is defined by a constellation of interconnected characteristics: (1) Nutrition: organisms take in food for energy and cellular maintenance; (2) Respiration: all organisms break down nutrients to release energy, where physical breathing (inhalation and exhalation via lungs, gills, skin, or leaf stomata) provides oxygen; (3) Irreversible Growth: cell multiplication leads to permanent increase in body size; (4) Excretion: removal of toxic metabolic wastes such as urea in animal urine, mineral salts in sweat, and excess water droplets excreted by plant leaves (guttation); (5) Response to Stimuli: organisms detect changes in their surroundings-such as human withdrawal from a sharp thorn, Mimosa pudica (touch-me-not/chhui-mui) folding upon touch, amla leaves closing at sunset, and seedling shoots bending toward light (phototropism) while roots grow toward gravity (geotropism); (6) Reproduction: organisms produce offspring of their own kind, ensuring species continuity; and (7) Lifespan and Death: living things complete their life cycle and eventually cease metabolic function. In seed biology, dry seeds exist in a dormant living state; when provided with water (to soften the seed coat and hydrate enzymes) and air/oxygen (to power embryonic respiration), the embryo resumes active growth. Bean seeds sprout in light or dark, while Coleus/Petunia need light and Calendula/Zinnia need dark. Sir Jagadish Chandra Bose used his Crescograph to demonstrate plant sensitivity and growth response to environmental stimuli. The chapter examines developmental transitions: the 5-stage life cycle of bean plants and complete metamorphosis in animals. Mosquitoes develop through 4 stages (Egg, aquatic Larva, active Pupa, and flying Adult); larvae breathe air at the surface via respiratory siphons, so applying kerosene oil blocks oxygen intake to suppress disease vectors. Frogs undergo metamorphosis from jelly-covered spawn into aquatic herbivorous tadpoles with gills and swimming tails, then into four-legged air-breathing froglets, and finally into carnivorous amphibious adults with strong leaping hind legs and moist skin/lung respiration.',
